krs 1
# NVIDIA SASS for sm_86, the machine code the device runs, read back by the cell's probes
# (test/engine/compiler/cell/cell_sass_probe*.c). The format is the comment at the head of ptx.krs. Every form below
# whose text is an instruction was read out of a probe's own listing: cell_ptx_probe assembles each membership
# question's kernel, written from ptx.krs, into a cubin, nvdisasm lists it, and what a question's listing holds past
# the frame's is its forms' machine code. The comment over each group names the question it was read from; a form
# whose instruction no question gave says so where it stands.
#
# The machine as the probes found it (25 questions, 33 operations, 0 failed, and each operation's fields from turning
# its 128 bits over one at a time):
#  - One file of 32-bit registers, R0 to R254, and RZ reading 0. The operand fields are Rd 16-23, Ra 24-31, Rb 32-39
#    and an immediate 32-63. There is no 64-bit register: a 64-bit value is two registers, and only an address or a
#    wide product needs them adjacent ([R2.64], IMAD.WIDE.U32 R2, R4, R3, ...).
#  - Predicates P0 to P6, and PT, which always holds. Three bits name one (81-83, 84-86 and 87-90, where 90 negates),
#    and every instruction carries a guard predicate at 12-15 (@P0, @!P0).
#  - A carry is a predicate, not a flag: IADD3 leaves it in the predicate it names, IADD3.X takes it back, and a
#    chain's last limb either keeps it (borrow_last) or drops it (add_last).
#  - Bits 105-127 are the scheduler's, not the operation's: a stall of 4, yield, a write barrier, a read barrier, a
#    wait mask of 6 and reuse of 4. This file writes none of them, and whatever assembles this text sets them.
#  - No instruction declares a register. How many a kernel holds is the ELF's (SHI_REGISTERS in the section header,
#    EIATTR_REGCOUNT in its attributes), so every declare_ form below is empty.
#  - The listings' own ABI: R1 holds the stack pointer (MOV R1, c[0x0][0x28]), a call leaves its return address in R20
#    and R21 (MOV R20, 32@lo(...), RET.ABS.NODEC R20 0x0), and a kernel's parameters lie in the constant bank from
#    c[0x0][0x160]. A lane written as a function leaves R1, R20 and R21 alone.
#
# What this file names that no listing prints, each one this file's own:
#  - .hi, the second register of a 64-bit pair. nvdisasm names that register itself (R3 for the pair at R2), and
#    whatever assembles this text resolves {r}.hi the same way. A form that needs both halves of a 64-bit value has no
#    other way to name the high one, because a ruleset writes a register from its number alone.
#  - The banks are all the one file, so a writer must give numbers that do not collide across them. The code generator
#    numbers each bank from 0 and needs an allocation before it writes SASS; the cell's probe gives its own numbers.
#  - The lane's fixed registers, reserved at the top of the file, and P6, this file's scratch predicate: the carry of
#    a chain, and the low half of a wide comparison. A lane of more than 240 registers, or of more than the three
#    predicates P0 to P2, is past what this file reserves.
#  - An immediate and an offset are written in decimal, as the code generator gives them, where a listing prints hex.
#  - A 64-bit form is two instructions, and holds only where its destination is neither of its sources: the first
#    instruction has written half the destination before the second reads them. A form's text can take no scratch to
#    stand between, so the writer gives a destination apart.
#
# What this file writes as a construct, because the part has the operation and no question ever made the compiler
# write it: predicate_xor and predicate_and. The compiler folded both into the ISETP that set the operand
# (ISETP.NE.U32.XOR P1, PT, R0, RZ, P0), so neither ever stood alone in a listing. Each is written 29 Sep out of forms
# the listings did give, which is what a construct is for.
#
# What this file does not write, each form left empty where it stands, with what the probes did see:
#  - word_divide and wide_divide. The part has no integer divide. For 32 bits the compiler writes a 14-instruction
#    reciprocal (I2F.U32.RP, MUFU.RCP, F2I.FTZ.U32.TRUNC.NTZ, IMAD.HI.U32, ...) over four scratch registers, and for
#    64 bits a CALL.ABS.NOINC to __cuda_sm20_div_u64, a function the cubin must hold. A form's text can take no
#    scratch, and only a construct can, whose lines must be forms this schema names.
#  - product_low and product_high. SASS writes both halves of a word product with one IMAD.WIDE.U32 into an aligned
#    pair, which is what the compiler did with the two together; neither half has an instruction of its own that also
#    carries the carry between them.
#  - count_add, launch_load, open_launch, shared_open, shared_close and program_unit: the lane's entry, its launch,
#    its file in shared memory and the resident kernel around it, none of which a question covered.
ruleset sass
toolchain cubin
header probe_nvdisasm

# the lane's registers, all of them the one file
bank file = R{n}
bank sign = R{n}
bank out = R{n}
bank atom = R{n}
bank temporary = R{n}
# the low register of a 64-bit pair; its high half is written {r}.hi
bank wide = R{n}
bank predicate = P{n}
bank member = R{n}
# a word's value written into a form, in decimal
bank immediate = {n}
fixed zero = RZ
fixed lane_number = R240
fixed record = R242
fixed index = R244
fixed body = R246
fixed bodies = R248
fixed tables = R250
fixed threads = R252
fixed sign_base = R253
fixed indexed = P3
fixed one = P4
fixed ok = P5

# the program's text around the lane. A listing's own opening is its .target and .elftype, which the header carries,
# and each function is a section of its own
form program_note steps major minor compiler_major compiler_minor block = // a record program of {steps} steps, for sm_{major}{minor}, NVRTC {compiler_major}.{compiler_minor}, as SASS for the target {block}\n
form lane_open = \n\t.section\t.text.cycle_lane,"ax",@progbits\n\t.align\t128\n\t.type\t\tcycle_lane,@function\ncycle_lane:\n
form lane_body = \n
form lane_close = .L_lane_end:\n\t.size\t\tcycle_lane,(.L_lane_end - cycle_lane)\n

# the lane's declarations: SASS has none, and how many registers the lane holds is the ELF's
nop declare_predicates count
nop declare_fixed_predicates
nop declare_file count
nop declare_signs count
nop declare_out count
nop declare_atoms count
nop declare_temporaries count
nop declare_wides count
nop declare_fixed_words
nop declare_fixed_wides
nop declare_members count

# the lane's opening and close. open_launch and launch_load are the lane's entry, which no question covered: a kernel
# read its parameters out of the constant bank (IMAD.WIDE.U32 R2, R4, R3, c[0x0][0x160]) and a function takes them by
# the ABI, and neither was probed
err open_launch
err launch_load to offset
# an address is global and generic alike here: PTX's cvta.to.global left no instruction in any listing
nop to_global to
err shared_open
err shared_close
# a load under a predicate (word_select, test_nonzero: @!P0 LDG.E.CONSTANT R7, [R2.64+0x4])
form guarded_load where to address = \t@{where} LDG.E.CONSTANT \t{to}, [{address}.64];\n
form guarded_widen where to from = \t@{where} MOV \t{to}, {from};\n\t@{where} MOV \t{to}.hi, RZ;\n
form open_error_unless where = \t@!{where} BRA \t`(.L_error_open);\n
form error where at = \t@{where} BRA \t`(.L_error{at});\n
form label_error_open = .L_error_open:\n
form label_error at = .L_error{at}:\n
err count_add address
# the lane is a function the resident calls, and returns through the address a call leaves in R20
form return = \tRET.ABS.NODEC R20 0x0;\n
form step_note at operation = \t// step {at}, operation {operation}\n

# carry chains through a register's limbs (add_alone; add_first, add_middle, add_last: 96 bits; subtract_alone;
# subtract_first, subtract_middle, subtract_last: 96 bits; borrow_alone, borrow_read; borrow_first, borrow_middle,
# borrow_last, borrow_read: 96 bits). A subtraction adds the right's negation, and takes its borrow back as ~right
form add_alone to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n
form add_first to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n
form add_middle to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, P6, {left}, {right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
form add_last to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
form subtract_alone to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, {left}, -{right}, RZ;\n
form subtract_first to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, -{right}, RZ;\n
form subtract_middle to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, P6, {left}, ~{right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
form subtract_last to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, {left}, ~{right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
# the subtract chain whose top limb leaves its borrow for borrow_read
form borrow_alone to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, -{right}, RZ;\n
form borrow_first to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, -{right}, RZ;\n
form borrow_middle to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, P6, {left}, ~{right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
form borrow_last to left right = \tIADD3.X \t{to}, P6, {left}, ~{right}, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
# the carry is 1 where the chain did not borrow, so -1 with it added is 0 there and all ones where it did
form borrow_read borrow where = \tIMAD.X \t{borrow}, RZ, RZ, -0x1, P6;\n\tISETP.NE.U32.AND \t{where}, PT, {borrow}, RZ, PT;\n

# a word's arithmetic (word_copy, word_set; word_and, word_or, word_xor; word_shift_left, word_shift_right;
# word_funnel_right; word_multiply, word_multiply_add; word_select, test_nonzero). A bitwise operation is one LOP3
# over a lookup of its three inputs: 0xc0 is a and b, 0xfc a or b, and 0x3c a xor b
form word_copy to from = \tMOV \t{to}, {from};\n
form word_set to value = \tMOV \t{to}, {value};\n
form word_and to left right = \tLOP3.LUT \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ, 0xc0, !PT;\n
form word_or to left right = \tLOP3.LUT \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ, 0xfc, !PT;\n
form word_xor to left right = \tLOP3.LUT \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ, 0x3c, !PT;\n
# a funnel shift of the pair (high, low) by the amount clamped to the width: a plain shift names RZ for the half it
# does not have, and .HI takes the result's high word
form word_shift_left to from bits = \tSHF.L.U32 \t{to}, {from}, {bits}, RZ;\n
form word_shift_right to from bits = \tSHF.R.U32.HI \t{to}, RZ, {bits}, {from};\n
form word_funnel_right to low high bits = \tSHF.R.U32 \t{to}, {low}, {bits}, {high};\n
form word_multiply to left right = \tIMAD \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n
form word_multiply_add to left right added = \tIMAD \t{to}, {left}, {right}, {added};\n
err word_divide to left right
form word_select to chosen otherwise where = \tSEL \t{to}, {chosen}, {otherwise}, {where};\n
# The two halves of a word product, which carry between them: the low is the product's low word plus the addend,
# keeping its carry in P6, and the high is the product's high word plus that carry. The compiler wrote the pair as
# one IMAD.WIDE.U32 into an aligned pair (form_13: MOV R7, RZ then IMAD.WIDE.U32 R6, R9, R0, R6), which needs the
# two destinations to be a pair; the core names them apart, so each is written on its own out of instructions every
# listing gave. Asked of the part 29 Sep, 0xffffffff squared plus 0xffffffff: the low answers 0 and the high
# 0xffffffff, as the question says
form product_low to left right added = \tIMAD \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n\tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {to}, {added}, RZ;\n
form product_high to left right = \tIMAD.HI.U32 \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n\tIMAD.X \t{to}, RZ, RZ, {to}, P6;\n

# a sign's arithmetic, a signed word of -1, 0 or 1 (sign_set, sign_select; sign_multiply, sign_absolute, sign_negate)
form sign_set to value = \tMOV \t{to}, {value};\n
form sign_select to chosen otherwise where = \tSEL \t{to}, {chosen}, {otherwise}, {where};\n
form sign_multiply to left right = \tIMAD \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n
form sign_absolute to from = \tIABS \t{to}, {from};\n
form sign_negate to from = \tIADD3 \t{to}, -{from}, RZ, RZ;\n

# tests, each setting a predicate (test_nonzero, test_zero, test_negative; test_signed_differ, test_signed_greater;
# test_wide_nonzero, test_wide_equal, test_wide_below, test_wide_below_and). One ISETP compares two words, ands its
# last operand into the answer, and .U32 reads them unsigned; a wide comparison is the low words compared, then the
# high words compared with .EX, which takes the low answer as its last operand. The listings gave ISETP.NE.U32.AND,
# ISETP.NE.AND, ISETP.GT.AND, ISETP.GE.AND, ISETP.GE.U32.AND, ISETP.LT.U32.AND and the .EX of NE, GE and LT, because
# the compiler read zero and below off the negations of NE and GE. Three spellings below no listing holds:
# ISETP.EQ.U32.AND, ISETP.EQ.U32.AND.EX and ISETP.LT.AND. They are not guessed from the others. The comparison sits
# in bits 76 to 78, unsigned in 73 and .EX in 72, and the probe's widening round turns each bit of a comparison the
# listings did hold and asks the disassembler what comes back: EQ.U32.AND is bit 78 of GE.U32.AND, EQ.U32.AND.EX is
# bit 78 of GE.U32.AND.EX, and LT.AND is bit 73 of LT.U32.AND. The part answers for all three
form test_nonzero where value = \tISETP.NE.U32.AND \t{where}, PT, {value}, RZ, PT;\n
form test_zero where value = \tISETP.EQ.U32.AND \t{where}, PT, {value}, RZ, PT;\n
form test_negative where value = \tISETP.LT.AND \t{where}, PT, {value}, RZ, PT;\n
form test_signed_differ where left right = \tISETP.NE.AND \t{where}, PT, {left}, {right}, PT;\n
form test_signed_greater where left right = \tISETP.GT.AND \t{where}, PT, {left}, {right}, PT;\n
form test_wide_nonzero where value = \tISETP.NE.U32.AND \tP6, PT, {value}, RZ, PT;\n\tISETP.NE.U32.AND.EX \t{where}, PT, {value}.hi, RZ, PT, P6;\n
form test_wide_equal where left right = \tISETP.EQ.U32.AND \tP6, PT, {left}, {right}, PT;\n\tISETP.EQ.U32.AND.EX \t{where}, PT, {left}.hi, {right}.hi, PT, P6;\n
form test_wide_below where left right = \tISETP.LT.U32.AND \tP6, PT, {left}, {right}, PT;\n\tISETP.LT.U32.AND.EX \t{where}, PT, {left}.hi, {right}.hi, PT, P6;\n
form test_wide_below_and where left right also = \tISETP.LT.U32.AND \tP6, PT, {left}, {right}, PT;\n\tISETP.LT.U32.AND.EX \t{where}, PT, {left}.hi, {right}.hi, {also}, P6;\n
# The two operations over predicates alone, each a construct: the part has one (PLOP3.LUT), and no question ever made
# the compiler write it, because it folded both into the ISETP that set the operand. So both go the long way, through
# the words a predicate selects, over forms every listing did give. An exclusive or is the cheaper of the two: it does
# not care that SEL reads its first operand from a register and so can only be given RZ, which selects 1 where the
# predicate is false, because negating both inputs leaves an exclusive or alone. An and is not, so it pays one MOV to
# hold the 1 and selects the predicates themselves
construct predicate_xor where left right
word_select {temporary:0} RZ 1 left
word_select {temporary:1} RZ 1 right
word_xor {temporary:0} {temporary:0} {temporary:1}
test_nonzero where {temporary:0}
end
construct predicate_and where left right
word_set {temporary:0} 1
word_select {temporary:1} {temporary:0} 0 left
word_select {temporary:2} {temporary:0} 0 right
word_and {temporary:1} {temporary:1} {temporary:2}
test_nonzero where {temporary:1}
end

# 64-bit words, each two registers (wide_from_word, word_from_wide, wide_pack, wide_unpack; wide_multiply,
# wide_multiply_word; wide_add, wide_add_unsigned; wide_shift_left, wide_select; wide_divide). A wide product writes
# both halves of a word product into the pair, and the cross products are added into the high half after it, so that
# the pair's write does not undo them
form wide_from_word to from = \tMOV \t{to}, {from};\n\tMOV \t{to}.hi, RZ;\n
form word_from_wide to from = \tMOV \t{to}, {from};\n
form wide_pack to low high = \tMOV \t{to}, {low};\n\tMOV \t{to}.hi, {high};\n
form wide_unpack low high from = \tMOV \t{low}, {from};\n\tMOV \t{high}, {from}.hi;\n
form wide_multiply to left right = \tIMAD.WIDE.U32 \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n\tIMAD \t{to}.hi, {left}.hi, {right}, {to}.hi;\n\tIMAD \t{to}.hi, {left}, {right}.hi, {to}.hi;\n
form wide_multiply_word to left right = \tIMAD.WIDE.U32 \t{to}, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n
form wide_add to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n\tIADD3.X \t{to}.hi, {left}.hi, {right}.hi, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
form wide_add_unsigned to left right = \tIADD3 \t{to}, P6, {left}, {right}, RZ;\n\tIADD3.X \t{to}.hi, {left}.hi, {right}.hi, RZ, P6, !PT;\n
# the high half reads both halves of the value shifted, and is written before the low half overwrites it
form wide_shift_left to from bits = \tSHF.L.U64.HI \t{to}.hi, {from}, {bits}, {from}.hi;\n\tSHF.L.U32 \t{to}, {from}, {bits}, RZ;\n
form wide_select to chosen otherwise where = \tSEL \t{to}, {chosen}, {otherwise}, {where};\n\tSEL \t{to}.hi, {chosen}.hi, {otherwise}.hi, {where};\n
err wide_divide to left right

# memory: the members' and the tables' words and the record's words (the frame's own loads and stores). An address is
# the pair at the register named, and .CONSTANT is PTX's ld.global.nc
form global_load to address offset = \tLDG.E.CONSTANT \t{to}, [{address}.64+{offset}];\n
form record_store offset from = \tSTG.E \t[R242.64+{offset}], {from};\n

# the states a target that clocks the lane a state at a time cuts it into (vhdl.krs); a SASS lane runs straight
# through, as a PTX one does, and has none
nop states_declare count
nop state_start state
nop state_open state
nop state_next state error
nop state_exit
nop dispatch_to error state

# a memory read's address named a state ahead of the read (vhdl.krs); a SASS load names its address itself
nop launch_ask offset
nop global_ask address offset
nop guarded_ask where address
nop count_ask address

# a loop: its label, and the branch back to it where the predicate holds
form loop_label loop = .L_loop{loop}:\n
form loop_back loop where = \t@{where} BRA \t`(.L_loop{loop});\n
nop state_loop back where next

# the program resident around the lane: the kernel the record machine launches. No question wrote it, and the probes
# read nothing of the shared memory, the barriers, the atomics or the timer it needs
err program_unit launch_bytes count_offset hot_offset block_offset number_offset ttl_offset checkin_every_offset next_offset start_offset finished_offset checkins_offset owner_at command_at state_at offset_at step_at launch_time_at exectime_at checkin_at checkin_time_at run_command stop_command done_state stopped_state yielded_state
